#N canvas 20 20 2900 1820 12;
#X text 20 20 u_root -- the actual root of Cut It. main.pd (on the device) and main-dev.pd (on the Mac) both instantiate this and nothing else \, which is what stops the two entry points drifting., f 58;
#X text 2300 20 creation arg 1: the Pd channel the nanoKONTROL's own channel 1 lands on -- 17 on the Organelle \, and on the Mac whatever slot it fills. creation arg 2: the same thing for the Launchpad -- 1 on the Organelle. Passed straight to m_nano and m_launchpad. creation arg 3: the DATA DIRECTORY for u_state \, absolute and with no trailing slash -- /sdcard/cut-it-state on the device and /tmp on the Mac. creation arg 4: the Pd channel the SP-404's own channel 1 lands on -- 33 on the Organelle \, passed to m_404. creation arg 5: the same for the Volca -- 49 \, passed to m_volca. creation arg 6: u_net's INBOUND UDP port -- 9001 on the device and 9002 on the Mac. Those six are the ONLY things main.pd and main-dev.pd are allowed to disagree about \, and args 3 and 6 are the two that actually do. ⚠️ Positional args cannot be skipped and Pd 0.49 does not warn about a wrong count -- see ref/architecture.md under Creation arguments. inlets: none. outlets: none., f 80;
#X text 20 230 THE AUDIO PATH IS WIRES \, ON PURPOSE. The four stages go in the gap below \, right to left \, so the chain stays something you can read off the canvas instead of tracing through send names., f 58;
#X obj 20 340 r~ inL;
#X obj 20 620 throw~ outL;
#X obj 330 340 r~ inR;
#X obj 330 620 throw~ outR;
#X obj 170 400 u_level in-l;
#X obj 480 400 u_level in-r;
#X text 70 490 v0.4: e_chop \, e_pitch \, e_trem \, e_verb go in this gap., f 30;
#X text 20 700 L = drums \, R = fx: the SP-404's hard-panned outputs arriving through the TRS Y-cable. adc~ 1 is the tip \, verified -- item 11., f 34;
#X text 20 800 adc~ and dac~ appear NOWHERE in this patch \, and must not. mother.pd owns both -- it publishes the inputs on inL / inR and collects the output from outL / outR \, then applies the volume knob and a limiter. A patch that writes to dac~ silently bypasses both. See C-4., f 58;
#X text 470 1050 The level taps are a SIGNAL fan-out \, which needs no trigger: signal connections all run every block \, so there is no firing order to get wrong. The trigger rule is about messages., f 46;
#X obj 900 340 u_init \$3;
#X text 1120 340 owns startup ORDER -- MIDI wiring \, the stage reports \, when the Launchpad is initialised and when saved state is restored. Outlet 1 fires at the launchpad stage \, outlet 2 at the last stage about 3.5s in., f 34;
#X obj 900 600 m_launchpad \$2;
#X text 1120 600 the Launchpad Pro MK3 \, and the only file allowed to talk to it: the Programmer/Live switch and the safe exit. Pads out on param and disp \, like any other device \, and surface ownership out of its one outlet., f 34;
#X obj 900 860 m_nano \$1;
#X text 1120 860 the nanoKONTROL. Decodes CC into named parameters -- every control including the six transport keys \, which are the mode selector from Phase 6 onward. It drives no bus itself: what a control MEANS is decided above this layer., f 34;
#X obj 900 1120 m_organelle;
#X text 1120 1120 the Organelle's own front panel \, mapped like any other device -- aux and the four knobs become named controls on param. Same layer as m_nano and the same silence about meaning., f 34;
#X obj 1700 340 u_err;
#X text 1700 390 the error bus. Anything raising a problem sends level \, source and text to err \; this decides what the mode lets through and forwards it to disp. It never draws -- g_oled owns the screen., f 34;
#X obj 1700 600 u_tempo;
#X text 1700 650 the master reference: master BPM \, the 24 PPQN pulse that MIDI clock is cut from \, and the transport. It is NOT the clock -- Cut It runs poly-tempo and a c_clock instance is what a beat comes from., f 34;
#X obj 1700 860 u_map \$3;
#X text 2000 860 THE MAP \, and the only file in Cut It that says what a control MEANS. Named controls in on param \, buses out. Organelle knob 1 is master tempo \, aux is the transport \, and the nano's transport row selects the mode., f 34;
#X obj 2300 340 g_oled;
#X text 2300 390 owns the OLED -- four layers \, each with a priority and a time to live. Everything else asks by sending to disp., f 34;
#X obj 2300 600 g_led;
#X text 2300 650 owns the aux button LED. Callers send led and a STATE on disp \, never a colour -- one writer per display surface \, and this is the surface that is not a screen., f 34;
#X text 2300 860 g_grid IS NOT IN THIS COLUMN \, and that is on purpose -- it sits at the bottom left \, next to the two boxes it is WIRED to. Where a file has cords \, the cords decide where it goes \; the column is only for the ones that talk on buses alone., f 34;
#X text 900 1300 THE COLUMNS ARE THE ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM. Left is the m_ layer \, device by device \; middle is the buses and the map \; right is the display owners. THE ONLY WIRES ON THIS CANVAS COME OUT OF u_init -- to m_launchpad and to u_state -- and they are wires rather than sends because the boot ORDER is u_init's while the ACTION is the other file's. That seam is worth being able to see \, and both would be invisible as a send. Everything else here talks on the allowlisted buses., f 90;
#X text 900 1420 RE-LAID OUT IN PHASE 6 \, with no behaviour change beyond the new box and the new wire. The audio chain has not moved. The control area was three ragged columns with cords that could not reach a new box without being drawn through a comment., f 90;
#X obj 20 1200 c_clock 1 8;
#X obj 20 1320 g_grid \$2;
#X text 300 1200 THE FIRST c_clock INSTANCE IN THE DEPLOYED PATCH \, and a real milestone: until now u_tempo published a master reference that nothing consumed. Ratio 1 \, eight beats to the bar \, so its beat-number outlet indexes the grid's bottom row directly. NOTHING DOWNSTREAM MAY ASSUME THE GLOBAL clock IS ITS CLOCK -- this instance is the grid's \, and the next consumer gets its own., f 58;
#X text 300 1360 owns the Launchpad's LEDs. Two wires in: ownership from m_launchpad \, so it goes silent the moment a panic hands the surface back \, and the beat number from the c_clock beside it., f 40;
#X obj 2300 1120 u_net 192.168.1.5 8000 \$6;
#X text 2300 1170 THE FOURTH DISPLAY SURFACE \, and the only file that talks to the phone. It CONSUMES disp like the two above it and owns no selector on it \, so unlike led in Phase 5 and grid in Phase 6 it cost g_oled's route nothing -- a mirror rather than a surface with a vocabulary. THE HOST IS A LITERAL ADDRESS AND IT IS ONLY RIGHT ON ONE NETWORK: the phone is 192.168.1.5 at home and 192.168.12.109 on the Organelle's own access point \, so THIS LINE HAS TO BE EDITED AND REDEPLOYED when the rig changes network. BROADCAST WAS TRIED AND REJECTED \, and the reason is worth keeping: 255.255.255.255 works -- Linux permits it \, PdParty accepts it \, and 19 to 20 of 20 datagrams arrive -- but WIFI ACCESS POINTS BUFFER BROADCAST FRAMES AND RELEASE THEM ON BEACON BOUNDARIES. Measured: unicast arrives every 200 ms as sent \, while broadcast arrives in bursts of three or four separated by up to 819 ms. THROUGHPUT IS IDENTICAL AND LATENCY IS NOT \, which is why a delivery test saw nothing and a person moving a knob saw it immediately. It also eats the phone's 1500 ms NO-LINK margin. ⛔ THE LINK IS BIDIRECTIONAL SINCE v0.3.5.1 AND THAT IS STILL NOT A WAY FOR THE PHONE TO ANNOUNCE ITSELF -- netreceive in 0.49 cannot tell you who sent a datagram \, which is why phone-ip.sh asks the Organelle's own DHCP lease instead. ARG 6 IS THIS END'S INBOUND PORT \, and the two cords out of this box are the Volca and the SP-404 \, exactly as u_map's are \, because no bus carries a sounding note. Why the address is discovered rather than configured \, and the broadcast measurements behind it \, are on ref/device/phone.md., f 58;
#X obj 1450 180 u_state \$3;
#X text 1450 250 THE DATA STORE. Two files under the directory in \$3 -- one flushed on a timer \, one written only on an explicit commit. It is wired rather than sent because the RESTORE is a stage of u_init's boot sequence \, exactly like the launchpad init beside it: u_init owns WHEN \, u_state owns WHAT. Everything else it does travels on the state bus., f 28;
#X text 1450 530 ⚠️ \$3 IS THE ONE THING THE TWO ENTRY POINTS MUST DISAGREE ABOUT \, and it is a real platform difference rather than an oversight: there is no /sdcard on a Mac. Data lives OUTSIDE the patch folder on purpose -- the same reasoning that put u_err's log there. The full contract is on ref/architecture.md under Creation arguments., f 28;
#X obj 1700 1150 m_volca \$5;
#X text 1700 1560 the Korg Volca FM \, and the FIRST OUTPUT-ONLY device layer in this patch -- it is told what to play rather than publishing what was touched. IT SITS HERE RATHER THAN IN THE m_ COLUMN because it has a cord \, and on this canvas the cords decide placement: it is wired straight up to u_map \, which is the only file allowed to decide that a note should sound. One cord \, selector-prefixed \, per C-2., f 44;
#X obj 2050 1400 m_404 \$4;
#X text 2050 1450 the SP-404MKII \, and the first BIDIRECTIONAL device layer -- it publishes pad presses on param and is told which pads to trigger through this cord. Wired to u_map for the same reason m_volca is \, and placed by its cord rather than by column., f 30;
#X obj 1450 900 u_present 4000 2000 33 8;
#X text 2600 900 DEVICE PRESENCE \, and the ONE bounded wire.sh recovery serving every device. It holds no per-device state -- a c_presence inside each pollable m_ decides whether ITS device is lost and this counts how many are \, so two cables out at once cannot double the fork rate. ⚠️ THE 4000 IS COUPLED TO u_init's LAST STAGE \, exactly like u_tempo's 4000: it must not tick before wire.sh has run once., f 34;
